{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact 66439e2af100e92c15fc833a0776833babaad1fe, tree b80053ca90e0f499ef1b7238562f42b3099055b7. Primary 3ea8f8b7 read first and in full (723 lines), then the complete docs/scopes.md Order delta 1037e7cb. All material findings from this review are below.\n\n1. Fidelity: the revised design addresses the prior review's intended directions: durable allocation finality, order-owned holes/expiry, retained original input, separate page/entry proof domains, enrollment after genesis, consistent binding/requirement language, and archiving from genesis. Source order remains a proposed generic tell form, and the late form is now owned by the companion packages note.\n2. Correctness: changes requested, F1–F5 below. In particular, moving resolution before commit removes expired effects but introduces an unfinished distributed commit between those steps.\n3. Evidence: design-only review, not runtime approval. Native artifact enumeration exhausted: the two fresh bindings at66439e2a are included; an older stale docs/scopes sibling is not this candidate. Authored delta against shared ancestor feccbbcf is exactly the new note and the Order paragraph: 2,118 regular leaves, all2,116 other leaves/modes/OIDs unchanged, whitespace clean, shared checkout clean. Base tree b5a0a28f1b0809bf5b090689761ff5557f3c5998; candidate tree b80053ca90e0f499ef1b7238562f42b3099055b7. Runtime/tests unchanged relative to that authored base: no repeated gate per docs/testing.md. The candidate does NOT include subsequent Main32b32fc2 changes; any later landing must preserve them. Scratch /private/tmp/checker-order-scope-draft2-i425g4hl/validation.json. An initial own validation compared against the wrong, newer Main and failed on the expected intervening changes; the corrected merge-base comparison above is the evidence. No source edits or provider/ref mutation.\n\nF1 (P1), lines318–359: persist and bind the exact prepared commit before asking the order scope to resolve it. The pending row retains only the input, digest, allocation and fact; resolution binds only member/P/input, not the local head, read witnesses, rule answers, clock, capacity reservation or resulting entry. Unsequenced inputs are explicitly allowed while pending. Counterexample: a sequenced transition is applied in the proposed judgment; resolve(P) commits at the order scope but its reply is lost; an unsequenced transition changes the item (or the input passes notAfter); restart resumes step4 and now judges refusal. The order scope already has resolved, so it cannot turn P into a hole, and no member entry exists. The ordinary head check/turn restart does not settle this. Specify a durable prepared result and the local-state/authority/time/reservation fence that permits exactly that result to commit, how a lost resolve/hole reply is recovered, and what changes may interleave. Bind the resolution to sufficient retained material to verify that entry. Require lost-resolution-reply plus local-change/clock/eviction controls, not only a lost allocation reply.\n\nF2 (P1), lines293–308, 354–364, 399–413, 521–528: resolved but uncommitted positions defeat the finite wait and staleness promises. Stop a member after the order scope records resolved and before step7. Expiry applies only to open, so that allocation never becomes a hole. A reader covering it must remain behind rather than use an older cut. Its wait can exceed lifetime forever if the member never returns, even with staleness \u003e= lifetime when the old allocation falls inside the cut. The one order clock correctly serializes expiry, but it cannot supply this missing member entry. State the actual failure/availability model and weaken the finite-wait claim, or design recoverable publication/finalization that supplies the exact committed result without that member returning. Do not expire a resolved allocation while the member may later commit. Update the companion packages note4.5 too.\n\nF3 (P1), lines388–440 and487–495: make genesis, holes and leave use one cut/absence rule in runtime and replay. The cut is defined as folding through genesis when no positioned entry exists, but Absence returns an empty page. A genesis that opens one ledger item has a nonempty fold, so the reader's empty page cannot pass the byte-for-byte replay check. Also all earlier allocations may be holed/expired with no positioned entry: runtime calls that absent:genesis, while replay requires no allocation at all. Finally5.5 calls a member absent when leave\u003c=X, but5.7 reads normally at X=L and makes it absent only above L. Choose whether genesis state is visible (or explicitly define a different initial cut), distinguish no allocation from all holes, define the exact inclusive fence, and make the retained witness/replay rules agree. Require nonempty-genesis, all-holed and X=L controls. Empty/no-entry must not stand in for an unresolved or missing dependency.\n\nF4 (P1), lines255–257, 455–477: closing a group must not strand its previously allocated positions. A member can have open P, then leave at L; leave expressly lets allocations at/belowL settle later. When every member has left, close is permitted and the scope is said to become terminal, even though P is still open or another resolved member entry is unpublished. Earlier-cut readers still require those positions. If terminal stops expiry/resolve/hole, the accepted duty never settles. Require all obligations to be settled and recoverable before terminal close, or explicitly keep their settlement/expiry available after close while refusing only new allocations. Cover leave/close racing a pending and a resolved-but-uncommitted input.\n\nF5 (P2), lines560–566, 601–605 and637: source order is high-water suppression, not arrival-order-independent effects. With a generic increment handler, delivering source pairs1 then2 applies both (count2); delivering2 then1 applies2 and supersedes1 (count1). The already-applied older effect cannot be superseded retroactively. Thus S1 cannot answer superseded to the lower pair in both arrival orders as written, and a shared order does not by itself make a relay trap end identically on every run: allocations are themselves admitted in a variable order (as5.8 correctly says). State the narrower contract—refuse a pair lower than one already applied—and name the exact observation whose equality the witness expects, or restrict the handler form enough to justify a stronger latest-value guarantee. Include the valid lower-first trace rather than testing only delayed-old delivery.\n\nPrior findings assessment: draft1 F1's lost-allocation hole and old-position retry counterexamples are addressed by order-journal finality and new positions after holes; the new resolution boundary is F1 here. F2's original-input retention is addressed, but the claimed finite progress is not, as F2 here shows. F3's byte-domain separation is addressed; absence/replay remains F3 here. F4's pre-genesis identity cycle is replaced by post-genesis enrollment. F5's definition/requirement/binding distinction and F6's genesis/archive-guide changes are addressed. F7's branch/refusal distinction is clearer, but the adopted string late:\"refuse\" still lacks its bound/reason in packages82f067e6; that issue is filed in the companion review. No runtime slice, broader group completion or hosted acceptance is approved.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3ea8f8b73fbd638adcd2b4c690e3cab1bafd5956","binding":"assigned","head":"66439e2af100e92c15fc833a0776833babaad1fe","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1dcf2ab5f9865872fe83871db936c706e052df41\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3e4a926151f6bdb386dade53b343c35eef2496dd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8afe6c0b3f698e0744da9f887cacea37c4f334c1","review_path":"reviewguard@